-- Sovietski: the Proletariat Light Machine and the Heavy Machine Combat Cyborg, WB36's restatements of the WB17 Light and Heavy Machines with their own M.D.C. by Location, held as new rows beside the WB17 ones (printed 128-133).
-- 2 vehicle rows, with 17 locations and 4 weapon entries.
--
-- One-off data script, run once per environment. NOT a migration - it adds
-- rows, not schema.
--
--   node scripts/d1-apply.mjs --local apps/character-creator/db/~394-sovietski-cyborgs-a.sql
--
-- Written by scripts/vessel-sql.mjs from 1 worker file(s). Every value
-- is printable ASCII, every row cites its book, and a Main Body location
-- agrees with its vehicle's mdc_main_body.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO vehicles (slug, name, system, vehicle_class, crew, passengers, speed_ground, speed_air, speed_water, dimensions, weight_tons, mdc_main_body, is_mega_damage, ar, cost, cost_note, description, source_book) VALUES
  ('sovietski-proletariat-light-machine', 'Sovietski Proletariat Light Machine Cyborg', 'rifts', 'borg', 'One human volunteer (full conversion).', 'None.', 'Running: 120 mph (192 km) maximum; 100 mph (160 km) in light battle armor, 85 mph (136 km) in medium. Leaping: 15 feet (4.6 m) high or 25 feet (7.6 m) lengthwise, +50% with a running start. With Cyber-Jet Nodes (50% of police and military units): jet assisted leaps of 150 feet (45.7 m) high or 300 feet (91.4 m) lengthwise.', 'None as standard; only by jet pack (80 M.D.C. for the Light Machine pack) or a bionic addition, acquired later or issued to the Red Star Reserve.', 'Swims about 15 mph (24 km); with an oxygen storage cell it can walk along a riverbed at about half that pace. Maximum depth 500 feet (152 m).', 'Height 6 feet (1.8 m); width 2 feet, one inch (0.64 m); length 1 foot (0.3 m).', '250 pounds (112.5 kg); heavily equipped police and military units up to 350 pounds (157.5 kg).', 170, 1, NULL, 1300000, 'Sovietski cost 1.3 million credits if manufactured; 2.2 million credits on the Black Market, fair availability. The Sovietski figure is stored.', 'Russia''s most-built light cyborg frame, a pre-Rifts Red Army design made by the tens of thousands and left sealed in warehouses the Cataclysm cut off. The Sovietski found nearly 100,000 of them raiding old bases and still turn up more each year; the early Warlords found thousands too, and the frame underlies most of their own cyborgs. Today it serves chiefly in reserve as a scout, spy, police and infantry body. It is about human size, so it can wear ordinary clothes, common body armor fitted to a six-foot human, or light or medium cyborg armor, and pass among civilians. Cyber-Docs like how simple it is to fix and improve: Cybernetic Medicine gets +10% when installing cybernetics or bionics in one, so long-serving frames often carry dozens of add-ons. Retiring Heavy and Superheavy Machines are usually moved into one; those who join the Red Star Reserve get 1D4+2 extra cybernetics and two extra bionic weapons of choice, and some active Superheavy Machines keep one as a second body to swap into. Civilian units: P.S. is throttled to the equal of Augmented Strength 28 (+13 to S.D.C. punch and kick damage; 1D4 M.D. from a power punch or leap kick, each using two attacks), Robot P.P. 17 (parry and dodge +1). A Body-Chop-Shop will lift the damper to Robot P.S. 24 for 8,000 credits, add a switch between Augmented and Robot P.S. for 15,000, and can raise P.P. to 20. Police and military units: Robot P.S. 24 (6D6 S.D.C. restrained punch, 1D4 M.D. full punch, 2D4 M.D. power punch, 1D6 M.D. kick, 2D6 M.D. leap kick; power punch and leap kick each use two attacks) and Robot P.P. 20 (parry and dodge +3); most can drop to Augmented P.S. at will so as not to kill an unarmored civilian. Nuclear, 20-year average life. Civilian standard features: targeting sight (+1 to strike) with light filters, 20/20 enhanced color vision, telescopic eye with passive nightvision, two multi-system eye sockets, universal headjack, language translator, radio of 5 miles (8 km), one secret leg compartment; a hand or forearm weapon for each hand/wrist and forearm only with government authorization (Red Star Reservists, permitted concealed carry, rural and border dwellers); no Red Star Light Machine armor without the same leave. Police and military standard features: the same sight and color vision, a super-telescopic eye with targeting sight, two multi-system eye sockets, universal headjack and finger jack, amplified audio, translator, 5-mile radio, concealed climb cord, two Cyberlink vehicle interfaces (hand and head), a Gromeko friend-or-foe computer, and a bionic lung with gas filter and oxygen cell; half have Cyber-Jet Nodes, 30% a camera or vid finger, 5% hydraulic leg boosters. They start with one forearm weapon of choice per arm besides the blades, two hand weapons or features of choice per hand, one leg feature or weapon per leg, a face of choice (police and Spetsnaz often add cyber-disguises, the Type AA-1 among them), and may swap a hand and forearm for a weapon appendage; they use the army''s heavier weapons and wear Red Star body armor, or the newer Light Proletariat environmental armor on assignment. Chop-shops, Warlord camps, Mindwerks and some mercenary outfits will fit these weapons to civilian frames for a price. The classic pre-Rifts military frame shown in the book''s art had a forearm Vibro-Blade over each wrist (50 M.D.C., 1D6 M.D.) and an ion blaster in one or both palms (3D6 M.D., 500 feet/152 m) fed by a standard E-Clip through a port under the forearm near the elbow.', 'Rifts World Book 36: Sovietski p.128-131'),
  ('sovietski-heavy-machine-combat-cyborg', 'Sovietski Heavy Machine Combat Cyborg', 'rifts', 'borg', 'One human volunteer (full conversion).', 'None.', 'Running: 100 mph (160 km) maximum, even in cyborg armor. Leaping: 15 feet (4.6 m) high or 25 feet (7.6 m) lengthwise, +50% with a running start. With Cyberjet Nodes: jet boosted leaps double running speed, and jet assisted leaps reach 100 feet (30.5 m) high or 200 feet (61 m) lengthwise.', 'Only with one of two elective options (most take one, some neither). Cyberjet Nodes (40%): hover up to 100 feet (30.5 m) for 1D4 minutes, then a gentle descent. SCJP-98 Cyborg Jet Pack (another 40%): 250 mph (400 km) maximum, cruising 70-100 mph (112 to 160 km), ceiling 2,000 feet (610 m).', 'Swims 20 mph (32 km); with an oxygen storage cell it walks along the floor of a river, lake or shallow sea at 8 mph (13 km). Maximum depth 600 feet (183 m).', 'Height 8-9 feet (2.4 to 2.7 m); width 3 feet (0.9 m) to 3 feet, 6 inches (1.1 m); length one foot, 6 inches (0.5 m) to 2 feet, 6 inches (0.8 m).', '600-900 pounds (270 to 405 kg), varying with load-out.', 240, 1, NULL, 2600000, 'Sovietski cost 2.6 million credits; 5.2 million credits on the Black Market, fair availability. The Sovietski figure is stored.', 'The Sovietski''s second most common cyborg: a 7 to 9 foot full conversion body kept mostly for the army and for the heaviest industrial work (dangerous factory jobs, mining, loading trucks and trains). Army units all look alike apart from unit marks and rank flourishes, always man-shaped and bearing the soldier''s own face or a synthetic copy; many take off the face plate, or the whole helmet, to show it. The government''s Cyborg Orientation Program keeps them tied to their humanity and country, and soldiers and citizens largely embrace them. They lack the fingers for delicate work. On retirement most are moved into a Proletariat Light Machine; many refuse and stay in the army or other approved work, and about 6% of Red Star Reservists keep their Heavy Machine bodies for distinguished service. Robot P.S. 32 and Robot P.P. 22: 1D4 M.D. restrained punch, 2D4 M.D. full punch, 4D4 M.D. power punch, 2D4 M.D. kick, 2D8 M.D. leap kick (power punch and leap kick each use two attacks). Bonuses: one extra attack per melee, initiative +1, strike, parry and dodge +4, pull punch +2. Penalties: -40% to Prowl for bulk, and -40% to Forgery, Picking Locks, Picking Pockets, surgery, Whittling/Sculpting, Art and any fine-fingered skill (tactile sensitivity is typically 35-50%). Red Nova cyborg armor adds 150 M.D.C. to its body and 50 to its head (light), 200 and 60 (medium), or 250 and 80 with helmet on (heavy); army units usually wear medium or heavy. They cannot wear human or Light Machine armor or clothes; clothing is made to order. Nuclear, 20-year average life. Standard features: targeting sight (+1 to strike) with light filters, 20/20 enhanced color vision, telescopic eye with passive nightvision, two multi-system eye sockets, universal headjack and fingerjack, amplified audio, language translator, 5-mile (8 km) radio, concealed climb cord, two Cyberlink vehicle interfaces (hand and head), Gromeko friend-or-foe computer, bionic lung with gas filter and oxygen cell, clock calendar and gyro-compass. Starting weapons and features: one weapon and one feature of choice per hand; the large forearm Vibro-Blade plus one ranged weapon of choice per forearm; one leg feature and one leg weapon per leg; a face of choice; two more head or eye features or weapons; and optionally a weapon appendage replacing a hand and forearm, rare on combat units. Chop-shops, Warlord camps, Mindwerks and some mercenaries will fit these to Shocktroopers or Heavy Machines for a price. Cyberjet Nodes sit in the back and lower legs: a lost leg node cuts jet speed and leaping by 10% each, a lost back unit by 80%, and with both gone the cyborg runs and leaps as normal. The SCJP-98 jet pack runs on an E-Pack (48 hours) or a cable to the cyborg''s power; after three hours of use every further 15 minutes is an even chance that it overheats and cuts out, and it wants 40 minutes, ideally an hour, to cool.', 'Rifts World Book 36: Sovietski p.131-133');

